sections.s2_technical:
Balance management uses double-entry bookkeeping: every operation produces two matching ledger entries so the system is always internally consistent and auditable. A top-up credits the user's wallet and debits a funding source account; a payment debits the payer and credits the payee. The balance is a derived value, but for performance it is also stored on the account row and protected with optimistic locking.

```java
// Optimistic locking: update succeeds only if version is unchanged
public boolean debit(String walletId, BigDecimal amount, long expectedVersion) {
    int rows = jdbc.update(
        "UPDATE wallets SET balance = balance - ?, version = version + 1 " +
        "WHERE id = ? AND version = ? AND balance >= ?",
        amount, walletId, expectedVersion, amount);
    return rows == 1;   // 0 means a concurrent update won → retry
}
```

Idempotency makes every operation safe to retry. Each top-up, payment, or transfer carries a client-generated idempotency key, and the server records the outcome under that key so a replay returns the same result rather than moving money twice. Peer-to-peer transfers must debit the sender and credit the receiver atomically, in a single database transaction, so a crash can never leave money debited from one side without crediting the other.

```java
// Atomic P2P transfer in one transaction
@Transactional
public void transfer(String from, String to, BigDecimal amount, String idemKey) {
    if (idempotency.seen(idemKey)) return;        // idempotent
    ledger.post(from, to, amount);                 // two matching entries
    walletRepo.debit(from, amount);                // both or neither (transaction)
    walletRepo.credit(to, amount);
    idempotency.record(idemKey);
}
```

Limits enforce daily and monthly caps per user, checked before each spend, which both satisfies regulation and limits fraud damage. Fraud detection scores transactions on velocity, amount, and device signals, blocking or challenging suspicious ones. Settlement and reconciliation run as scheduled jobs: settlement moves funds with partner banks, and reconciliation compares the wallet ledger against gateway statements to catch mismatches. A senior nuance is partial-failure handling: if a top-up succeeds at the payment gateway but the local database write fails, the money is in limbo, so the reconciliation job detects the gateway credit without a matching ledger entry and repairs it, never silently losing the customer's money.

